<%- include('partials/header') %>

<section class="section prose">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p>
    This page explains what's held constant across benchmark runs, what each
    metric means, and where results can legitimately differ. Read it before
    comparing numbers across configurations — Vulkan vs SYCL and llama.cpp vs
    vLLM are not measuring quite the same thing, and the raw flags attached
    to every result exist so you can judge that for yourself.
  </p>
  <p class="section-note">
    Arrived with a specific problem? Skip to
    <a href="#faq">the questions this page answers</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="matrix">The test matrix</h2>
  <p>
    vLLM has no Vulkan backend on Intel GPUs (SYCL/XPU only), so the real
    matrix is three runtime×backend combinations, not a full 2×2 — each run
    across both cards and three crossed axes: a concurrency sweep from
    single-stream up through fairly saturated batching, a context-length
    sweep, and a prefill-length sweep:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Runtime</th><th>Backend</th><th>Cards</th><th>Concurrency</th><th>Context</th><th>Prefill</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>llama.cpp</td><td>Vulkan</td><td>B70, B65</td><td>1, 2, 4, 8, 16</td><td>8K, 64K, 128K</td><td>256, 7936</td></tr>
        <tr><td>llama.cpp</td><td>SYCL</td><td>B70, B65</td><td>1, 2, 4, 8, 16</td><td>8K, 64K, 128K</td><td>256, 7936</td></tr>
        <tr><td>vLLM</td><td>SYCL</td><td>B70, B65</td><td>1, 2, 4, 8, 16</td><td>8K, 64K, 128K</td><td>256, 7936</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Concurrency 1 is the single-session case; 2 through 16 are the parallel-
    session case — the same sweep answers both questions rather than treating
    "single vs. parallel" as a separate axis.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Context</strong> is the server's KV budget
    (<code>--ctx-size</code> / <code>--max-model-len</code>);
    <strong>prefill</strong> is how many prompt tokens each request actually
    sends. They're separate axes because they stress different things — a
    large context costs VRAM whether or not it gets filled, while a long
    prefill is what actually loads the prefill path and moves
    <em>prompt eval tok/s</em>. The short prefill (256 tokens) is a chat-shaped
    request; the long one (7936) is a document-shaped one. It is 7936 rather
    than a round 8192 so that it still fits alongside the 256 tokens it
    generates at the 8K context level — a round number there would silently
    drop that whole row.
  </p>
  <p>
    A cell runs only when there's room for the prefill plus the 256 tokens it
    generates — <code>context ≥ prefill + 256</code>, on both runtimes.
    Concurrency doesn't enter that test on either side: vLLM's
    <code>--max-model-len</code> is a per-request ceiling, and the llama.cpp
    runs use a unified KV cache, so the context budget is shared rather than
    carved into per-slot shares. With the levels above, every combination
    fits.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a non-default sweep does produce an unrunnable combination, it is
    <strong>skipped rather than truncated</strong> — a request whose prompt
    doesn't fit would silently measure something else — and listed in the
    run's summary. A missing row in the results table means "not runnable,"
    not "failed."
  </p>

  <h2 id="models">Models benchmarked</h2>
  <p>
    Three models, each run across the whole matrix. They are chosen so that
    the two things that most move throughput on a 32 GB card vary one at a
    time — dense against mixture-of-experts, and one vendor's architecture
    against another's. All three fit on a single card at 4-bit, which is the
    point of the card.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model</th><th>Architecture</th><th>llama.cpp</th><th>vLLM</th><th>Why it's here</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% MODELS.forEach(function(m) { %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= m.name %></td>
            <td><%= m.architecture %></td>
            <td><%= m.llamacppQuant %></td>
            <td><%= m.vllmQuant %></td>
            <td><%= m.note %></td>
          </tr>
        <% }) %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Results on other models are welcome and publishable — the
    <a href="/submit">submission form</a> takes any model name. These three
    are simply what this site's own sweeps run, so they are the ones with
    complete coverage across every card, backend and runtime.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recipes">Recipes</h2>
  <p>
    Every flag that isn't one of the three swept axes comes from a named
    <a href="/recipes">recipe</a> — a small, hand-picked set of launch
    scripts, three profiles per runtime and backend. The benchmark runner
    sources those same files rather than carrying its own tuned command, and
    each result records which recipe produced it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That arrangement is deliberate: it means the script a reader copies is the
    script that was measured, and a recipe can't quietly drift away from the
    numbers offered as evidence for it. The only things the runner overrides
    are the swept axes, the port and model, greedy decoding (so run-to-run
    variance comes from the hardware rather than the sampler), and
    <code>--metrics</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="setup">Reproducing this</h2>
  <p>
    The full setup — drivers, both llama.cpp builds, the vLLM XPU
    environment, which card is active, and how power is measured — is written
    up in <code>docs/SETUP.md</code> in the repository, with a command that
    proves each step. The benchmark tooling itself lives in
    <code>scripts/bench/</code>, and <code>REMOTE_AGENT_PROMPT.md</code> there
    is a self-contained brief you can hand to an agent running on the
    benchmark machine.
  </p>

  <h2 id="held-constant">What's held constant</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Model/quant is not forced to match across runtimes.</strong>
      llama.cpp and vLLM typically use different model formats entirely
      (GGUF quants like Q4_K_M vs. AWQ/GPTQ/FP8/unquantized) — this site
      doesn't pretend those are the same thing. What <em>is</em> held
      constant is the model/quant used for a given runtime across its own
      concurrency sweep and both cards, so single-vs-parallel and
      B70-vs-B65 comparisons stay valid within that runtime. Always check a
      result's flags (every row has them) before comparing across runtimes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Repeats.</strong> Each cell in the matrix is run 4 times; the
      first is discarded as a warmup (JIT/cache effects) and the published
      number is the median of the remaining three. All raw per-repeat output
      is kept in each result's raw log, not just the median. Four rather than
      three, because with three the "median" of the two scored runs is just
      their mean — it rejects no outliers while still being called a median.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Context is a shared budget, not a per-slot share.</strong>
      The llama.cpp runs use a unified KV cache
      (<code>-kvu</code>), so <code>--ctx-size</code> is one pool the
      parallel slots draw from as they need it, rather than a total divided
      evenly among them. Adding concurrency therefore doesn't shrink each
      session's context ceiling, and "context length" in the results table
      means the same thing at every concurrency level. vLLM's
      <code>--max-model-len</code> is a per-request ceiling, which behaves
      the same way for this purpose — though the two flags reach it
      differently, so it is still not a like-for-like number across runtimes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Throughput is aggregate, and measured the same way on both
      runtimes.</strong> <em>Generation tok/s</em> is total output tokens per
      second of wall time across all parallel sessions — the number that
      should climb as concurrency rises. It is not the rate a single session
      sees, which falls. Getting this consistent took some care: llama-server
      also exposes counters whose ratio is a mean <em>per-slot</em> rate, and
      vLLM exposes an "input throughput" that is really input tokens per
      second of benchmark. Neither is used. The per-slot rate is kept
      alongside, in each row's raw log, because it is a useful number in its
      own right — just not the same one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Card selection is operator-confirmed, not auto-detected.</strong>
      Nothing in the benchmark tooling inspects which physical GPU is
      active — whoever runs it explicitly labels each run B70 or B65 and is
      responsible for making sure that's actually true (physical swap, or a
      device-selector env var when both are installed at once).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tooling">Tooling per runtime</h2>
  <p>
    Each runtime is measured with its own real serving path rather than a
    synthetic micro-benchmark, and the same tool is used across the whole
    concurrency sweep for a given runtime — no mixing measurement methods
    within one runtime's numbers:
  </p>
  <dl>
    <dt>llama.cpp</dt>
    <dd>
      A real <code>llama-server</code> instance (continuous batching on,
      <code>--metrics</code> enabled) is started per context length ×
      concurrency level (both are launch-time flags), then hit with that many
      concurrent completion requests at each prefill length in turn. Prompts
      are synthetic filler calibrated to the target prefill length against the
      server's own <code>/tokenize</code> endpoint, and the pool is sized to
      at least twice the concurrency level with each worker starting at its
      own offset — so no two requests in flight at once share a prompt, and
      prefix caching can't quietly serve most of a concurrent batch from
      cache. Token counts come from the server's own <code>/metrics</code>
      counters rather than from the client, which avoids counting HTTP
      overhead as GPU work; they are divided by the client's wall-clock window
      to give aggregate throughput. Client-side counting is used only as a
      fallback if the metrics names aren't found, and it measures the same
      quantity, so a fallback row stays comparable.
    </dd>
    <dt>vLLM</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>vllm bench serve</code> against a running <code>vllm serve</code>
      instance, at each concurrency level via <code>--max-concurrency</code>
      and each prefill length via <code>--dataset-name random
      --random-input-len</code> (rather than a natural-text dataset, whose
      prompt lengths aren't controllable — the cost is that prompt content is
      synthetic, the benefit is that prefill length is directly comparable to
      the llama.cpp side) — vLLM's own standard benchmarking tool, which
      already accounts for its continuous-batching scheduler correctly.
      Generation throughput is its <code>output_throughput</code>, which is
      already aggregate. Prefill throughput is derived from mean time-to-first
      -token rather than from its <code>input_throughput</code> field, which
      measures input tokens per second of benchmark and so falls as output
      length rises. Under concurrency, TTFT includes queue wait ahead of
      prefill, so the figure understates prefill speed slightly — exact at
      concurrency 1, conservative above it.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="metrics">Metrics</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Prompt eval tok/s</dt>
    <dd>Prefill throughput — how fast the prompt itself is processed before generation starts, per second of <em>prefill</em> time rather than per second of benchmark. Matters most for long-prefill workloads (document summarization, RAG), which is what the 8192-token prefill level is there to measure; read it alongside the prefill column rather than on its own.</dd>
    <dt>Generation tok/s</dt>
    <dd>Decode throughput — tokens produced per second once generation is underway. The number most people mean by "tokens per second." Reported as an <strong>aggregate</strong>: the total across every parallel session, not the rate one session sees. It should rise with concurrency while per-session speed falls; the per-session figure is kept in each row's raw log.</dd>
    <dt>tok/s per watt</dt>
    <dd>Generation tok/s divided by measured GPU power draw during the run. Only reported when power was actually measured — treat a missing value as "not measured," not zero. This site's own figures are the mean board draw over the load window, with no idle baseline subtracted; a submitted figure measured some other way is still useful, but read its notes before comparing it to one of ours.</dd>
    <dt>Context length</dt>
    <dd>The KV-cache budget the server was started with, not the number of tokens actually used. Higher costs VRAM up front and generally lowers generation throughput. It is a shared budget rather than a per-slot share, so it means the same thing at every concurrency level — see <a href="#held-constant">what's held constant</a>.</dd>
    <dt>Prefill</dt>
    <dd>Prompt tokens sent per request. Blank on runs submitted before this axis existed, or where the submitter didn't control prompt length — treat it as "unknown," not zero.</dd>
    <dt>Concurrency</dt>
    <dd>Number of parallel sessions/requests served simultaneously. 1 = single-stream latency (favors llama.cpp's design goals); higher values measure aggregate throughput under load via continuous batching (favors vLLM's design goals). Comparing runtimes at only one concurrency level tells an incomplete story.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="vulkan-vs-sycl">Vulkan vs SYCL</h2>
  <p>
    In practice this is a comparison of Intel's oneAPI/Level Zero compute
    path (SYCL) against the generic Vulkan compute path. SYCL is often
    ahead on raw throughput when it's stable and built correctly; Vulkan is
    generally more portable and less finicky to get building in the first
    place. Every result page shows driver/SDK versions and a stability
    column (crashes, required kernel flags) alongside throughput — a fast
    number from an unstable build isn't a fair win.
  </p>

  <h2 id="runtimes">llama.cpp vs vLLM</h2>
  <p>
    These two runtimes optimize for different things: llama.cpp is tuned
    for single/low-concurrency latency, vLLM for throughput under
    concurrent load via continuous batching. Results are captured at both a
    single-stream concurrency and at higher concurrency levels where
    possible — filter the <a href="/results">results table</a> by
    concurrency to see each runtime on its own turf rather than assuming
    one number tells the whole story.
  </p>

  <h2 id="submissions">Submissions</h2>
  <p>
    Every result on this site — including the maintainer's own — goes
    through the same <a href="/submit">submission form</a> and is manually
    verified before it's published. See the submit page for the exact
    fields captured and a copyable prompt you can hand to your own AI agent
    to help fill them in accurately.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions this page answers</h2>
  <p>
    Phrased the way people actually hit them. Each answer points at the
    section or the filtered results behind it.
  </p>
  <div class="faq-list">
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>"Should I build with Vulkan or SYCL?"</summary>
      <p>
        There isn't one answer, which is why both are benchmarked here
        rather than one being declared the winner. SYCL is often ahead on
        raw throughput when it builds cleanly and stays stable; Vulkan is
        usually the less finicky path to a working setup. Compare them on
        your own model and concurrency:
        <a href="/results?backend=SYCL">SYCL results</a> ·
        <a href="/results?backend=Vulkan">Vulkan results</a>, and read
        <a href="#vulkan-vs-sycl">Vulkan vs SYCL</a> for what the comparison
        does and doesn't establish.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>"My SYCL build is slower than Vulkan on the same card."</summary>
      <p>
        Both backends are sensitive to what's underneath them, so the first
        thing to check is the stack, not the runtime. Every row on the
        <a href="/results">results table</a> expands to show the exact
        kernel, GPU driver, oneAPI/Vulkan SDK and runtime version behind the
        number — find a row that beats yours on the same card and backend
        and compare versions against it. If your setup is genuinely slower
        on a newer stack, that's worth
        <a href="/submit">submitting</a>: a slow result with full versions
        attached is data, not noise.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>"Why did my tok/s drop when I raised concurrency?"</summary>
      <p>
        Check which number you're looking at first. Per-session throughput is
        expected to fall as concurrency rises; aggregate throughput is the
        number that should climb, and aggregate is what this site publishes.
        If your own figure comes from llama-server's
        <code>tokens_predicted_total / tokens_predicted_seconds_total</code>,
        that ratio is a mean per-slot rate — roughly flat across a concurrency
        sweep — not aggregate throughput, and it isn't comparable to the
        numbers here. See
        <a href="#held-constant">throughput is aggregate</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        What won't explain it: per-slot context shrinking. The runs here use a
        unified KV cache, so adding parallel slots doesn't divide the context
        budget between them.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>"Can I compare a llama.cpp number against a vLLM one?"</summary>
      <p>
        Not directly, and this site doesn't pretend otherwise. The two
        runtimes load different artifacts entirely — GGUF quants like
        Q4_K_M versus AWQ/GPTQ/FP8 — so a cross-runtime gap mixes the
        runtime, the quantization and the model file together. What is
        comparable is a runtime against itself across cards, backends and
        concurrency levels. See
        <a href="#held-constant">what's held constant</a> and
        <a href="#runtimes">llama.cpp vs vLLM</a>.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>"Is the B65 worth it over the B70?"</summary>
      <p>
        Not enough B65 data has been published here to answer that
        honestly yet — check
        <a href="/results?card=B65">what exists so far</a> before treating
        any B65 figure as representative. Benchmarking on a B65 is the
        single most useful thing anyone can
        <a href="/submit">contribute</a> right now.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>"Can I trust a number on this page?"</summary>
      <p>
        Judge it from what's attached to it rather than taking it on faith.
        Every row publishes the exact command that produced it, the card,
        backend, runtime and their versions, the quantization, context and
        concurrency, whether the run crashed, and the raw per-repeat log
        where one was supplied. Published figures are the median of repeated
        runs with a warmup discarded (<a href="#held-constant">repeats</a>),
        and results with power measured also carry tok/s per watt. A number
        missing its conditions is one you should discount — including here.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>"A result here doesn't match what I measure."</summary>
      <p>
        Likely and worth reporting. Card labels are operator-asserted rather
        than auto-detected (<a href="#held-constant">card selection</a>), the
        stack moves quickly enough that a months-old row may simply be
        stale, and prompt length and context both shift the numbers. Send
        what you measured through the <a href="/submit">submission form</a>
        with your versions attached — a contradicting result with full
        conditions is more useful than a correction with none.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

